Q: Would that be a good idea to make tuples like initializer_list of different types? A: <p>I really like C#'s ValueTuple syntax sugar -- they have trivial syntax to manifest one (a, b) with deduced types.  You can also give synax sugar names to the individual elements (the underlying type doesn't have those names, but a compiler transform handles it seamlessly)</p>
<p>In C++ world, if somethign were ever adopted, might look like (I am sure the language experts would tell me what I suggest here is ambiguous in many ways, so read for general intent.  C# uses parentheses, I am using square brackets to behave similarly to structured bindings):</p>
<pre><code>int a = 7
double b = 10.0
std::string c = &quot;hello&quot;

auto tuple = [a, b, c] // constructs std::tuple&lt;int, double, std::string&gt;

std::cout &lt;&lt; tuple.c // compiler transforms to the equivilent of std::get&lt;2&gt;(tuple)

//Really handle for ad hoc multi return values

auto get_values() {
    std::string foo = &quot;foo&quot;;
    return [1, foo];
}

auto [d, e] = get_values(); // structured bindings decompose to int, std::String

//-----------------
// some more interesting scenarios
std::string c1 = &quot;hello &quot;;
std::string c2 = &quot;world&quot;;
int a = 7;
double b = 3.0;

// since &quot;c1+c2&quot; is not a valid name, we can provide one using
// something similar to designated initializers
auto tuple = [a, .b=3.0, .c=c1+c2]; 
std::cout &lt;&lt; tuple.c 

// parameter is std::typle&lt;int, double, std::string&gt; const &amp;
void process_values([int d, double e, std::string f] const &amp;item) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; item.d &lt;&lt; item.e &lt;&lt; item.f;
    auto [d, e, f] = item; // structured bindings work as expected
}

// even though the &quot;names&quot; are different, tuple2 is still
// std::tuple&lt;int, double, std::string&gt; so compatible with process_values
auto tuple2 = [a, b, c1]; 
process_values(tuple);  
process_values([1, 4.0, c1]);
</code></pre>

Q: How can I display label as Latex math formula? A: <p>You can't use MathJaX or any kind of (La)TeX processing with chart.js titles,
legend labels or axis titles and labels: they are pure text drawn with the
canvas API, not even HTML. In fact you can't apply any kind of styling for parts
of those strings, like subscripts or italics.</p>
<p>You can however use MathJaX with external HTML titles and legends. See
<a href="https://www.chartjs.org/docs/latest/samples/legend/html.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this sample</a>
from the docs for how to make an external legend (while titles are trivial --
just align your html title on top of the canvas).</p>
<p>With HTML legend, an important issue to take into consideration is the fact that you
have to allocate the space for the legend yourself, to place the legend
where it's visible and not overlapping the plot.</p>
<p>Here's the example from the documentation link above, with small changes and
the addition of MathJaX processing.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>MathJax = {
    tex: {
        inlineMath: {'[+]': [['$', '$']]}
    }
};
const getOrCreateLegendList = (chart, id) =&gt; {
    const legendContainer = document.getElementById(id);
    let listContainer = legendContainer.querySelector('ul');

    if (!listContainer) {
        listContainer = document.createElement('ul');
        listContainer.style.display = 'flex';
        listContainer.style.flexDirection = 'column';
        listContainer.style.margin = 0;
        listContainer.style.padding = 0;

        legendContainer.appendChild(listContainer);
    }

    return listContainer;
};

const htmlLegendPlugin = {
    id: 'htmlLegend',
    afterUpdate(chart, args, options) {
        const ul = getOrCreateLegendList(chart, options.containerID);

        // Remove old legend items
        while (ul.firstChild) {
            ul.firstChild.remove();
        }

        // Reuse the built-in legendItems generator
        const items = chart.options.plugins.legend.labels.generateLabels(chart);

        items.forEach(item =&gt; {
            const li = document.createElement('li');
            li.style.alignItems = 'center';
            li.style.cursor = 'pointer';
            li.style.display = 'flex';
            li.style.flexDirection = 'row';
            li.style.marginTop = '5px';

            li.onclick = () =&gt; {
                const {type} = chart.config;
                if (type === 'pie' || type === 'doughnut') {
                    // Pie and doughnut charts only have a single dataset and visibility is per item
                    chart.toggleDataVisibility(item.index);
                } else {
                    chart.setDatasetVisibility(item.datasetIndex, !chart.isDatasetVisible(item.datasetIndex));
                }
                chart.update();
            };

            // Color box
            const boxSpan = document.createElement('span');
            boxSpan.style.background = item.fillStyle;
            boxSpan.style.borderColor = item.strokeStyle;
            boxSpan.style.borderWidth = item.lineWidth + 'px';
            boxSpan.style.display = 'inline-block';
            boxSpan.style.flexShrink = 0;
            boxSpan.style.height = '20px';
            boxSpan.style.marginRight = '10px';
            boxSpan.style.width = '20px';

            // Text
            const textContainer = document.createElement('p');
            textContainer.classList.add('legend-label');
            textContainer.style.color = item.hidden ? '#aaa' : item.fontColor;
            textContainer.style.margin = 0;
            textContainer.style.padding = 0;
            //textContainer.style.textDecoration = item.hidden ? 'line-through' : '';

            const text = document.createTextNode(item.text);
            textContainer.appendChild(text);

            li.appendChild(boxSpan);
            li.appendChild(textContainer);
            ul.appendChild(li);
        });
        const doc = MathJax.startup?.document;
        if(doc){
            //doc.reset();
            MathJax.startup.document.state(0);
            MathJax.texReset();
            doc.render();
        }
    }
};

const NUM_DATA = 7;
const data = {
    labels: Array.from({length: NUM_DATA}, (_, i) =&gt; new Date(`2000-${i+1}-01`).toLocaleString('default', { month: 'long' })),
    datasets: [
        {
            label: '$e^x$',
            data: Array.from({length: NUM_DATA}, ()=&gt;Math.floor(Math.random()*100)),
            borderColor: '#ff0000',
            backgroundColor: '#ff000080',
            fill: false,
        },
        {
            label: '$e^{x^2}$',
            data: Array.from({length: NUM_DATA}, ()=&gt;Math.floor(Math.random()*100)),
            borderColor: '#0000ff',
            backgroundColor: '#0000ff80',
            fill: false,
        },
    ],
};

const config = {
    type: 'line',
    data: data,
    options: {
        plugins: {
            htmlLegend: {
                // ID of the container to put the legend in
                containerID: 'legend-container',
            },
            legend: {
                display: false,
            }
        }
    },
    plugins: [htmlLegendPlugin],
};

new Chart(document.getElementById('chart'), config);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div style="border:1px solid black"&gt;
    &lt;h3 style="text-align: center;margin: 0; padding: 0"&gt;Comparaison de $e^x$ et $se(x)$&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 5em"&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            &lt;canvas id="chart"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div id="legend-container"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@4/tex-mml-chtml.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
